- Jae Seok.

1475
00:52:09,176 --> 00:52:10,976
Here. Wipe it off.

1476
00:52:10,976 --> 00:52:12,846
It looks like I ordered a separate dish of bulgogi.

1477
00:52:13,576 --> 00:52:14,646
Everything fell out.

1478
00:52:15,516 --> 00:52:17,246
- Goodness.
- Should I order a bowl of rice?

1479
00:52:18,216 --> 00:52:20,346
- Yes? Jae Seok.
- Dong Hwi.

1480
00:52:20,586 --> 00:52:21,616
I thought you came to a bulgogi place.

1481
00:52:21,616 --> 00:52:22,716
Seoul Institute of the Arts.

1482
00:52:22,916 --> 00:52:25,556
Even though I dropped out, we're still alumni.

1483
00:52:27,126 --> 00:52:29,726
(He's back to tacos.)

1484
00:52:30,356 --> 00:52:32,266
It's good. This sauce is tasty.

1485
00:52:32,326 --> 00:52:33,366
It's good.

1486
00:52:34,166 --> 00:52:35,166
(Amazed)

1487
00:52:35,666 --> 00:52:37,296
- It's good.
- How is it, Se Jong?

1488
00:52:37,296 --> 00:52:38,336
- It's good.
- Yes.

1489
00:52:39,436 --> 00:52:41,406
See? I said we should get tacos.

1490
00:52:42,606 --> 00:52:44,006
(Biting)

1491
00:52:44,006 --> 00:52:45,146
I was hungry.

1492
00:52:45,146 --> 00:52:47,576
- I love it.
- I'm going to modify it.

1493
00:52:47,576 --> 00:52:50,316
(The flavors of Mexico fill up their mouths.)

1494
00:52:52,186 --> 00:52:54,186
- It's delicious.
- Come on.

1495
00:52:54,186 --> 00:52:55,756
(Jae Seok is still dropping food.)

1496
00:52:55,756 --> 00:52:57,786
Did you bring spare clothes today?

1497
00:52:59,026 --> 00:53:02,426
(He's lost in the food even when the others tease him.)

1498
00:53:02,796 --> 00:53:04,126
How's Rita, Yeon Seok?

1499
00:53:04,296 --> 00:53:05,496
She's doing great.

1500
00:53:05,496 --> 00:53:07,726
She's so pretty and such a good girl.

1501
00:53:08,136 --> 00:53:11,266
Jae Seok, can you find my cat?

1502
00:53:11,636 --> 00:53:14,006
- You made them with LEGO blocks.
- Yes, I did.

1503
00:53:14,006 --> 00:53:15,006
Gosh. It's been a while.

1504
00:53:15,006 --> 00:53:16,006
(Showing off whenever possible)

1505
00:53:16,406 --> 00:53:18,706
- Did you make them with blocks?
- Yes.

1506
00:53:18,706 --> 00:53:20,206
Se Jong, will you take a look at it?

1507
00:53:21,716 --> 00:53:23,076
Come on, Se Jong.

1508
00:53:23,076 --> 00:53:24,246
When he's focused on something...

1509
00:53:24,246 --> 00:53:26,116
- He's focused on eating.
- He's too busy eating.

1510
00:53:26,116 --> 00:53:28,286
- It's time for him to eat.
- But they're so cute.

1511
00:53:28,656 --> 00:53:29,856
(Paying belated attention)

1512
00:53:29,856 --> 00:53:31,486
Don't swipe it with your hand like that.

1513
00:53:31,486 --> 00:53:32,786
No. That's not it.

1514
00:53:33,326 --> 00:53:35,626
In my opinion, he doesn't care.

1515
00:53:36,126 --> 00:53:38,326
What interests you the most these days?

1516
00:53:38,496 --> 00:53:40,766
What interests me the most? The script for my next work.

1517
00:53:40,826 --> 00:53:42,066
- I see.
- That's true.

1518
00:53:42,066 --> 00:53:44,666
Have you started watching your weight?

1519
00:53:45,206 --> 00:53:47,306
After breaking my ankle before,

1520
00:53:47,306 --> 00:53:50,306
I'm trying to stay in the same weight range.

1521
00:53:50,776 --> 00:53:52,006
How did you break your ankle?

1522
00:53:52,006 --> 00:53:54,106
It was a stress fracture.

1523
00:53:54,106 --> 00:53:55,716
I guess it couldn't bear the weight.

1524
00:53:56,246 --> 00:53:59,216
It can happen. How old are you, Se Jong?

1525
00:53:59,486 --> 00:54:00,616
I'm 35.

1526
00:54:00,786 --> 00:54:02,486
Around that age,

1527
00:54:02,486 --> 00:54:04,216
- your body starts to wear down.
- That's right.

1528
00:54:04,486 --> 00:54:07,256
Back then, I got hurt while shooting an action scene...

1529
00:54:07,256 --> 00:54:09,126
or doing something big.

1530
00:54:09,126 --> 00:54:10,356
But nowadays, I get hurt...

1531
00:54:10,356 --> 00:54:11,926
- just by waking up.
- That's true.

1532
00:54:12,126 --> 00:54:14,896
- You're right. It's over nothing.
- You're flustered.

1533
00:54:14,996 --> 00:54:16,096
That's sad.

1534
00:54:16,796 --> 00:54:17,796
Just because.

1535
00:54:18,006 --> 00:54:20,106
I could go on forever if I were to talk about that.

1536
00:54:20,106 --> 00:54:21,106
Of course.

1537
00:54:21,106 --> 00:54:22,576
Do you run, Se Jong?

1538
00:54:22,576 --> 00:54:24,746
Yes. I ran early this morning too.

1539
00:54:24,906 --> 00:54:26,446
I woke up at 4:40am.

1540
00:54:26,676 --> 00:54:28,816
I'm saying this seriously.

1541
00:54:28,816 --> 00:54:30,146
At 4:40am?

1542
00:54:30,416 --> 00:54:32,046
You look tired.

1543
00:54:32,146 --> 00:54:33,286
(Haggard)

1544
00:54:33,286 --> 00:54:35,616
Gosh. You woke up at 4:40am.

1545
00:54:35,616 --> 00:54:36,956
How much did you run?

1546
00:54:37,286 --> 00:54:39,186
- About 6.5km.
- About 6.5km.

1547
00:54:39,386 --> 00:54:40,626
It rained too heavily.

1548
00:54:40,626 --> 00:54:42,726
- I know. It poured in the morning.
- Did you run through the rain?

1549
00:54:43,226 --> 00:54:44,426
Do you run on rainy days too?

1550
00:54:44,496 --> 00:54:45,996
I usually run in the evening.

1551
00:54:46,126 --> 00:54:48,236
- Right.
- When my whole body is awake.

1552
00:54:48,236 --> 00:54:50,666
I didn't look good when I looked in the mirror this morning.

1553
00:54:50,666 --> 00:54:53,106
You should take a bath at times like that.

1554
00:54:53,436 --> 00:54:54,506
So...

1555
00:54:55,106 --> 00:54:56,336
- Did you hear that?
- Yes.

1556
00:54:57,536 --> 00:54:58,606
- Dong Hwi.
- What?

1557
00:54:58,606 --> 00:54:59,846
(Snapped for a moment)

1558
00:54:59,846 --> 00:55:01,276
I said taking a bath would...

1559
00:55:01,276 --> 00:55:02,946
But Dong Hwi...

1560
00:55:02,946 --> 00:55:04,416
- Dong Hwi.
- I'm saying this...

1561
00:55:04,616 --> 00:55:06,716
because he insists on doing one thing.

1562
00:55:07,046 --> 00:55:09,556
- I take baths.
- I want to thank you today...

1563
00:55:09,556 --> 00:55:11,456
because we had these tacos thanks to you.

1564
00:55:12,026 --> 00:55:14,356
It was because you backed me up proactively.

1565
00:55:14,696 --> 00:55:16,026
No wonder people adore him.

1566
00:55:16,026 --> 00:55:18,196
In the end, I was the one who found this place.

1567
00:55:19,066 --> 00:55:22,466
People adore you too, but in an uneasy way.

1568
00:55:23,836 --> 00:55:25,506
- Why?
- It's easy to adore him.

1569
00:55:25,806 --> 00:55:27,566
- I found this place.
- Look at Se Jong.

1570
00:55:27,836 --> 00:55:29,806
- And I brought you here.
- You make me feel uneasy.

1571
00:55:29,806 --> 00:55:31,176
It doesn't feel nice and clean.

1572
00:55:32,906 --> 00:55:34,976
- How should we do this?
- What should we do?

1573
00:55:34,976 --> 00:55:36,176
You changed the subject smoothly.

1574
00:55:36,176 --> 00:55:37,176
What about this?

1575
00:55:37,616 --> 00:55:39,416
What? You crush a piece of nacho.

1576
00:55:39,416 --> 00:55:41,216
You crush it into pieces,

1577
00:55:41,216 --> 00:55:43,056
and the one with the fewest pieces pays.

1578
00:55:43,216 --> 00:55:45,126
- The one with the fewest crumbs.
- You squeeze it once.

1579
00:55:45,486 --> 00:55:48,026
Let's play rock-paper-scissors, and pick the nacho piece.

1580
00:55:48,356 --> 00:55:49,956
- One of these?
- Yes.

1581
00:55:49,956 --> 00:55:51,666
- I think this is enough.
- That sounds about right.

1582
00:55:51,666 --> 00:55:52,796
Rock-paper-scissors.

1583
00:55:52,796 --> 00:55:54,366
(Paper, scissors, scissors, scissors)

1584
00:55:54,466 --> 00:55:56,596
(Dong Hwi is in 4th place.)

1585
00:55:56,596 --> 00:55:57,736
- I want to be third.
- Rock-paper-scissors.

1586
00:55:58,206 --> 00:55:59,936
- I won.
- Rock-paper-scissors.

1587
00:56:00,536 --> 00:56:02,136
- Gosh.
- I'm in first place.

1588
00:56:02,136 --> 00:56:03,136
I'll pick this one.

1589
00:56:03,576 --> 00:56:04,606
I'm in second place, right?

1590
00:56:04,606 --> 00:56:06,646
- It's better to pick a large piece.
- That's right.

1591
00:56:06,646 --> 00:56:08,116
(It's better to pick a large piece that's easier to crush.)

1592
00:56:08,116 --> 00:56:09,816
But this one is...

1593
00:56:09,916 --> 00:56:10,916
(Dong Hwi got the smallest piece.)

1594
00:56:10,916 --> 00:56:12,416
- What should I do?
- We can't help you.

1595
00:56:12,416 --> 00:56:13,946
- Do we use one hand only?
- Yes.

1596
00:56:14,186 --> 00:56:16,916
- Okay. Are you ready?
- Here. Are you ready?

1597
00:56:17,616 --> 00:56:19,386
I can't help it. We're all set.

1598
00:56:19,826 --> 00:56:21,856
- Okay. In 1, 2, 3.
- In 2, 3.

1599
00:56:22,026 --> 00:56:23,026
(Crushing)

1600
00:56:27,966 --> 00:56:29,666
- What's the order?
- Hold on.

1601
00:56:29,666 --> 00:56:30,866
(Squeezing with all they've got)

1602
00:56:30,866 --> 00:56:32,366
Okay. I'll go first.

1603
00:56:32,466 --> 00:56:33,566
You can go first.

1604
00:56:33,966 --> 00:56:35,736
- What? You'll pay.
- What? It's just one piece.

1605
00:56:35,736 --> 00:56:37,206
- What?
- I got two pieces.

1606
00:56:37,206 --> 00:56:38,946
- Gosh. It's driving me nuts.
- You'll pay for lunch.

1607
00:56:38,946 --> 00:56:40,006
There are only two pieces.

1608
00:56:40,346 --> 00:56:41,406
(Yeon Seok will reveal his next.)

1609
00:56:41,406 --> 00:56:42,776
- Let's ignore the tiny pieces.
- Look.

1610
00:56:43,046 --> 00:56:44,316
I have more than you did.

1611
00:56:44,316 --> 00:56:45,516
- You got 3 pieces.
- They are 3 pieces.

1612
00:56:45,516 --> 00:56:47,546
- We have more than you, Jae Seok.
- He got five.

1613
00:56:47,546 --> 00:56:48,616
- Yes.
- Five pieces.

1614
00:56:49,316 --> 00:56:51,056
- Four pieces.
- That's more than expected.

1615
00:56:51,786 --> 00:56:53,956
(Jae Seok will pay.)

1616
00:56:55,826 --> 00:56:58,266
Why did you move the pieces at the end?

1617
00:56:59,096 --> 00:57:00,796
I wanted to put them in the same place.

1618
00:57:01,536 --> 00:57:02,896
(Taking it out on the wrong person)

1619
00:57:02,896 --> 00:57:04,596
You ruined it by moving the pieces.

1620
00:57:04,766 --> 00:57:05,766
Thank you, Jae Seok.

1621
00:57:05,766 --> 00:57:07,266
- I mean... Yes.
- I'll pay for lunch.

1622
00:57:07,266 --> 00:57:08,936
(Today, Jae Seok pays.)

1623
00:57:08,936 --> 00:57:10,076
We enjoyed it so much.

1624
00:57:10,276 --> 00:57:12,606
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1625
00:57:12,606 --> 00:57:13,646
- Thank you.
- Yes.

1626
00:57:14,376 --> 00:57:16,176
- Thank you, Jae Seok.
- Thank you.

1627
00:57:16,176 --> 00:57:17,616
- Thank you.
- Yes.

1628
00:57:17,746 --> 00:57:19,386
- Let's go this way.
- Shall we?

1629
00:57:20,686 --> 00:57:22,456
Why don't we get some coffee?

1630
00:57:22,456 --> 00:57:23,456
Sure. Let's do that.

1631
00:57:23,456 --> 00:57:24,926
(A coffee break in the middle)

1632
00:57:25,126 --> 00:57:27,086
This place has nice coffee.

1633
00:57:27,486 --> 00:57:29,026
- Have you been here before?
- Yes.

1634
00:57:29,026 --> 00:57:30,356
Across from here...

1635
00:57:30,496 --> 00:57:31,996
Their coffee is pretty nice too.

1636
00:57:32,766 --> 00:57:35,266
Should we go to the second floor of this cafe?

1637
00:57:35,266 --> 00:57:37,036
- Shall we?
- They have a good bakery selection.

1638
00:57:37,036 --> 00:57:38,466
- Let's go there.
- It has blue ribbons.

1639
00:57:38,466 --> 00:57:39,666
Yes. They have a great bakery.

1640
00:57:39,666 --> 00:57:41,606
Gosh. It has changed completely.

1641
00:57:42,206 --> 00:57:43,406
- What?
- Gosh.

1642
00:57:43,976 --> 00:57:46,076
- It feels like overseas.
- But it's not open yet.

1643
00:57:46,306 --> 00:57:48,006
- Hello.
- It opens at 12pm. Wait.

1644
00:57:48,006 --> 00:57:49,546
It's 1 minute to 12pm.

1645
00:57:50,146 --> 00:57:52,646
- It'll open in 2 minutes.
- Should we wait for 1 minute?

1646
00:57:52,646 --> 00:57:54,246
- This place is good.
- We'll be the first customers.

1647
00:57:54,856 --> 00:57:57,016
Right. We happen to be the first customers.

1648
00:57:57,516 --> 00:57:58,586
We're first in line.

1649
00:57:59,086 --> 00:58:00,326
Gosh. Look at that.

1650
00:58:01,656 --> 00:58:04,496
- Gosh.
- They have a pastry chef.

1651
00:58:04,726 --> 00:58:05,726
It looks delicious.

1652
00:58:05,726 --> 00:58:06,726
(Trotting)

1653
00:58:06,766 --> 00:58:08,596
It opens at 12pm sharp.

1654
00:58:09,236 --> 00:58:10,296
(It opens right on the dot.)

1655
00:58:10,296 --> 00:58:11,706
They open at 12pm sharp.

1656
00:58:11,706 --> 00:58:12,966
- Hello.
- Hello. Yes.

1657
00:58:12,966 --> 00:58:15,276
- Gosh. They look delicious.
- Come on. Gosh.

1658
00:58:15,276 --> 00:58:17,436
- Then we should...
- They look so pretty.

1659
00:58:18,576 --> 00:58:22,876
(The sweet-looking desserts catch their eyes.)

1660
00:58:23,916 --> 00:58:25,046
(Magnetic)

1661
00:58:25,046 --> 00:58:27,216
- They look so pretty.
- Why don't you pay for these too?

1662
00:58:27,216 --> 00:58:29,486
(Reading his face)

1663
00:58:29,616 --> 00:58:30,656
That's a bit...

1664
00:58:30,656 --> 00:58:33,386
(Laughing awkwardly)

1665
00:58:33,786 --> 00:58:34,896
Sure.

1666
00:58:35,326 --> 00:58:36,926
(Patting)

1667
00:58:36,926 --> 00:58:38,796
Why don't you? I mean...

1668
00:58:39,726 --> 00:58:41,126
The way you laughed...

1669
00:58:41,426 --> 00:58:44,166
I've never heard you laugh like that.

1670
00:58:44,196 --> 00:58:45,306
- He was...
- It's because I'm thrilled.

1671
00:58:45,906 --> 00:58:47,666
I laughed because I loved to pay for it.

1672
00:58:48,036 --> 00:58:49,476
- I love it.
- Or shall we draw cards?

1673
00:58:49,476 --> 00:58:50,936
- What?
- Let's draw cards.

1674
00:58:50,936 --> 00:58:53,046
- Should we do that?
- Let's have her pick a card.

1675
00:58:53,146 --> 00:58:54,706
- Good. I like that. Wait.
- Yes.

1676
00:58:55,046 --> 00:58:56,876
- You have the same cards.
- They're the same.

1677
00:58:56,876 --> 00:58:58,446
Do you even use the same card?

1678
00:58:58,646 --> 00:59:00,316
- What are you two doing right now?
- Nothing.

1679
00:59:00,716 --> 00:59:02,016
- What?
- We even have the same cards.

1680
00:59:02,516 --> 00:59:04,556
- How is this possible?
- That's interesting.

1681
00:59:05,056 --> 00:59:06,556
- Good.
- What do you want?

1682
00:59:06,556 --> 00:59:07,586
- Let's order drinks first.
- Pick drinks first.

1683
00:59:07,586 --> 00:59:09,396
I'll get a Sidney Sunrise.

1684
00:59:09,396 --> 00:59:12,396
I'll try a black sesame cream latte.

1685
00:59:12,466 --> 00:59:13,966
What? A black sesame latte?

1686
00:59:14,626 --> 00:59:16,096
- I...
- I'll get the black sesame latte.

1687
00:59:16,096 --> 00:59:17,566
(2 black sesame lattes)

1688
00:59:17,666 --> 00:59:19,206
I'll get an iced Americano.

1689
00:59:19,206 --> 00:59:20,866
I'll also get an iced Americano.

1690
00:59:21,836 --> 00:59:22,906
- And...
- And...

1691
00:59:22,906 --> 00:59:24,506
(Next, they'll order desserts.)

1692
00:59:24,776 --> 00:59:26,906
- A fig eclair.
- Yes. I'll get that one.

1693
00:59:26,906 --> 00:59:28,416
I'll get the strawberry-and-basil hat.

1694
00:59:28,516 --> 00:59:29,846
One choconut, please.

1695
00:59:30,146 --> 00:59:31,446
I'll get this one.

1696
00:59:31,446 --> 00:59:32,986
- A chocolate eclair.
- A chocolate eclair.

1697
00:59:33,616 --> 00:59:36,086
- Okay.
- Gosh. It costs as much as lunch.

1698
00:59:37,986 --> 00:59:39,026
Se Jong.

1699
00:59:39,026 --> 00:59:40,086
(Robot Yang is genuinely surprised.)

1700
00:59:40,086 --> 00:59:41,656
- You might pay for it.
- Gosh.

1701
00:59:42,756 --> 00:59:44,426
(The stakes are higher than expected.)

1702
00:59:44,426 --> 00:59:45,466
- When you flip them like that...
- I mean...

1703
00:59:45,466 --> 00:59:46,996
- Hold on.
- Don't rig it.

1704
00:59:48,126 --> 00:59:49,596
(Feeling guilty)

1705
00:59:49,596 --> 00:59:51,906
- What's wrong with you?
- Hold on.

1706
00:59:52,236 --> 00:59:53,706
I wouldn't rig it, would I?

1707
00:59:53,706 --> 00:59:55,406
Now, will you pick one?

1708
00:59:55,406 --> 00:59:56,636
You can pick whatever you want.

1709
00:59:56,636 --> 00:59:58,346
(It's time to draw the card.)

1710
00:59:58,346 --> 00:59:59,706
You can pick whatever.

1711
01:00:00,276 --> 01:00:02,416
- Please pick the nicest one.
- Yes. The nicest one.

1712
01:00:02,416 --> 01:00:04,246
(Choosing the card next to it)

1713
01:00:06,716 --> 01:00:08,316
(Obviously, it's his card.)

1714
01:00:08,316 --> 01:00:09,856
But why did you...

1715
01:00:10,316 --> 01:00:11,856
Gosh. It costs as much as lunch.

1716
01:00:11,856 --> 01:00:13,626
It's okay. It's all right, Jae Seok.

1717
01:00:13,626 --> 01:00:15,826
- Of course.
- If it's for you,

1718
01:00:15,826 --> 01:00:17,356
- Thank you.
- Thanks.

1719
01:00:17,356 --> 01:00:18,896
I can do anything.

1720
01:00:18,896 --> 01:00:20,496
- Thanks, Dong Hwi.
- Sure.

1721
01:00:20,496 --> 01:00:22,336
As long as you can stay healthy,

1722
01:00:23,036 --> 01:00:24,036
I'd spend every last penny.

1723
01:00:24,036 --> 01:00:26,166
(They're seated.)

1724
01:00:26,166 --> 01:00:27,336
Should we try them?

1725
01:00:27,966 --> 01:00:31,006
It looks really tasty. Gosh.

1726
01:00:31,976 --> 01:00:33,746
Gosh. I broke it again.

1727
01:00:34,376 --> 01:00:36,576
(Laughing)

1728
01:00:37,976 --> 01:00:39,016
(Satisfied)

1729
01:00:39,946 --> 01:00:42,086
- Gosh. It's so good.
- It's really tasty.

1730
01:00:42,516 --> 01:00:45,186
Try this, Jae Seok. It's so...

1731
01:00:45,586 --> 01:00:47,956
- Try mine.
- Okay. It's so good.

1732
01:00:49,126 --> 01:00:50,156
(Savoring)

1733
01:00:50,556 --> 01:00:52,396
Yeon Shik loves stuff like this.

1734
01:00:53,326 --> 01:00:54,366
It's delicious.

1735
01:00:56,166 --> 01:00:59,066
(Savoring elegant desserts, forgetting how full he is)

1736
01:00:59,066 --> 01:01:00,106
It looks good.

1737
01:01:01,906 --> 01:01:03,436
It's really good.

1738
01:01:03,676 --> 01:01:04,806
It's so good.

1739
01:01:05,106 --> 01:01:07,606
- This is really good, isn't it?
- Actually... It's so good.

1740
01:01:10,316 --> 01:01:11,416
Gosh. It tastes so good.

1741
01:01:11,886 --> 01:01:14,316
I'm curious about the crust.

1742
01:01:14,316 --> 01:01:15,356
- It's so good.
- What is it?

1743
01:01:15,356 --> 01:01:16,956
I don't know. It might be ice cream. It's so good.

1744
01:01:16,956 --> 01:01:18,456
(The four of them are immersed in the sweet paradise.)

1745
01:01:19,426 --> 01:01:21,526
- It's so good.
- I see. It melts.

1746
01:01:22,096 --> 01:01:24,026
- Gosh. Darn.
- Jae Seok.

1747
01:01:25,326 --> 01:01:26,326
(The big brother never changes.)

1748
01:01:26,326 --> 01:01:27,326
There it is.

1749
01:01:27,326 --> 01:01:30,096
Jae Seok, Yeon Seok's and my place are close to here.

1750
01:01:30,096 --> 01:01:31,466
Should we grab some clothes you can change into?

1751
01:01:32,306 --> 01:01:34,666
I should clean myself up over there.

1752
01:01:34,666 --> 01:01:35,876
(Who is Whenever Guest they'll meet after this sweet break?)

1753
01:01:36,206 --> 01:01:38,476
- It's from Lee Se Woong.
- Se Woong.

1754
01:01:39,276 --> 01:01:41,146
What? "Real men wear pink."

1755
01:01:42,646 --> 01:01:45,316
He says real men wear pink.

1756
01:01:45,316 --> 01:01:47,916
- Real men wear pink.
- I see.

1757
01:01:47,916 --> 01:01:49,656
"To become a real man,"

1758
01:01:49,656 --> 01:01:52,086
"I've collected pink items whenever possible."

1759
01:01:52,086 --> 01:01:53,886
"I live for pink and die for pink."

1760
01:01:54,586 --> 01:01:56,526
"Lately, I've been defeated by pink's nemesis,"

1761
01:01:56,696 --> 01:01:59,126
"which is fat."

1762
01:02:00,096 --> 01:02:02,796
"Please support me, so I can become a pink boy again."

1763
01:02:04,136 --> 01:02:05,636
- Fat?
- So, he...

1764
01:02:05,636 --> 01:02:07,206
- has gained a lot of weight.
- So... Right.

1765
01:02:07,206 --> 01:02:09,606
He can no longer wear the pink items...

1766
01:02:09,606 --> 01:02:11,406
- he has collected.
- That's right. Yes.

1767
01:02:11,406 --> 01:02:12,406
I'm curious.

1768
01:02:13,346 --> 01:02:15,416
(It's a 10-minute walk.)

1769
01:02:15,416 --> 01:02:16,646
You're handsome.

1770
01:02:16,646 --> 01:02:17,916
Thank you.

1771
01:02:17,916 --> 01:02:19,046
Gosh. Thank you.

1772
01:02:19,046 --> 01:02:20,916
- You were talking about me, right?
- Yes.

1773
01:02:20,916 --> 01:02:22,756
His eyes definitely met mine.

1774
01:02:22,816 --> 01:02:25,756
You guys always get compliments on your looks.

1775
01:02:25,756 --> 01:02:26,856
You should've answered him.

1776
01:02:28,386 --> 01:02:29,856
Turn right. We'll turn right.

1777
01:02:29,856 --> 01:02:30,956
(The original navigator gives smooth directions.)

1778
01:02:30,956 --> 01:02:33,496
- Gosh. The weather's perfect.
- What a beautiful day.

1779
01:02:33,496 --> 01:02:34,626
- Gosh. Hello.
- Hello.

1780
01:02:35,236 --> 01:02:36,736
Gosh. You're handsome.

1781
01:02:36,736 --> 01:02:37,736
Did you see that?

1782
01:02:37,866 --> 01:02:39,866
- I was alone this time.
- You were, Jae Seok.

1783
01:02:39,866 --> 01:02:40,866
(Finally, he got the compliment all alone.)

1784
01:02:40,866 --> 01:02:42,136
It was a compliment just for you.

1785
01:02:42,706 --> 01:02:45,606
- Yesterday, I was in Ikseon-dong, - Okay.

1786
01:02:46,246 --> 01:02:48,106
and I got lots of compliments.

1787
01:02:48,446 --> 01:02:50,376
Someone even said my head was the size of a pea.

1788
01:02:50,976 --> 01:02:53,216
- I mean, how big could a pea be?
- My head...

1789
01:02:53,216 --> 01:02:54,216
(Unforgettable compliment)

1790
01:02:54,216 --> 01:02:57,186
- Should we go down this way?
- I was told it was pea-sized.

1791
01:02:57,186 --> 01:02:58,656
It's smaller than a fist now.

1792
01:03:00,856 --> 01:03:02,556
- I'm curious about these houses.
- Which ones?

1793
01:03:02,556 --> 01:03:04,296
I'm curious about these structures...

1794
01:03:04,296 --> 01:03:05,626
where there are shops on the first floor,

1795
01:03:05,626 --> 01:03:07,626
and there are houses upstairs.

1796
01:03:08,166 --> 01:03:09,436
- I see.
- They intrigue me.

1797
01:03:09,436 --> 01:03:11,036
- We just passed...
- Mixed-use buildings?

1798
01:03:11,036 --> 01:03:14,006
- But we passed this way earlier.
- Mixed-use buildings.

1799
01:03:14,006 --> 01:03:15,706
I was looking at the map back then.

1800
01:03:15,706 --> 01:03:17,476
You didn't get to look around.

1801
01:03:17,536 --> 01:03:19,106
(Two hours ago,)

1802
01:03:19,106 --> 01:03:20,976
(he didn't get to look around the alleyway...)

1803
01:03:20,976 --> 01:03:22,816
(because he was focused on the map.)

1804
01:03:23,816 --> 01:03:25,446
(He finally gets to look around.)

1805
01:03:25,446 --> 01:03:26,786
We went past here.

1806
01:03:26,986 --> 01:03:28,246
- We went past it this morning.
- I see.

1807
01:03:28,246 --> 01:03:29,616
- Hello.
- Hi.

1808
01:03:29,616 --> 01:03:31,456
We don't know...

1809
01:03:31,456 --> 01:03:33,356
- when the trend will change again.
- That's right.

1810
01:03:33,356 --> 01:03:35,456
- It's tough.
- From the back,

1811
01:03:35,626 --> 01:03:37,496
Se Jong looks like a student...

1812
01:03:38,156 --> 01:03:39,726
who's heading to the library.

1813
01:03:40,096 --> 01:03:42,996
Se Jong is showing me many aspects of him.

1814
01:03:43,296 --> 01:03:44,896
- He's very steadfast.
- Yes.

1815
01:03:44,896 --> 01:03:47,836
- He's really...
- He's quiet and funny.

1816
01:03:47,836 --> 01:03:49,636
- He's a real deal.
- He's so unique.

1817
01:03:49,636 --> 01:03:50,736
He's the real package.

1818
01:03:50,736 --> 01:03:52,106
- I mean...
- It's my second time seeing him...

1819
01:03:52,106 --> 01:03:53,376
since "Running Man."

1820
01:03:53,376 --> 01:03:54,606
When he's on a show,

1821
01:03:54,606 --> 01:03:56,916
I can't remember anything particular he excels at,

1822
01:03:56,916 --> 01:03:58,076
but he's hilarious.

1823
01:03:58,416 --> 01:04:01,416
- He doesn't really speak.
- He doesn't talk much.

1824
01:04:01,586 --> 01:04:03,456
- But he's funny.
- But he's hilarious.

1825
01:04:04,056 --> 01:04:05,926
He has this strange charm.

1826
01:04:05,926 --> 01:04:06,986
- Se Jong.
- Yes?

1827
01:04:06,986 --> 01:04:08,326
I don't want you to change.

1828
01:04:08,326 --> 01:04:09,326
(They're all mesmerized by Se Jong.)

1829
01:04:09,326 --> 01:04:11,096
Se Jong, have you been on other variety shows?

1830
01:04:11,696 --> 01:04:15,696
Well, I've done many part-time jobs since I was young.

1831
01:04:15,696 --> 01:04:17,336
- I see.
- Yes.

1832
01:04:17,336 --> 01:04:18,736
- I meant other variety shows.
- Variety shows.

1833
01:04:18,736 --> 01:04:19,836
- Other variety shows?
- Se Jong.

1834
01:04:19,836 --> 01:04:21,306
- Other...
- Other shows I've been on?

1835
01:04:21,306 --> 01:04:22,636
I looked at him from the back and...

1836
01:04:22,836 --> 01:04:26,076
On the variety show where you worked at a cafe...

1837
01:04:26,076 --> 01:04:27,876
- I didn't hear you right.
- What did you think I said?

1838
01:04:27,876 --> 01:04:30,076
I thought you asked, "Have you done other works?"

1839
01:04:30,076 --> 01:04:31,646
(The more they see Se Jong, the more interesting he gets.)

1840
01:04:31,646 --> 01:04:32,746
Gosh. It was hilarious.

1841
01:04:33,916 --> 01:04:35,116
- Jae Seok.
- Yes?

1842
01:04:35,416 --> 01:04:38,116
- There's a pink building.
- What?

1843
01:04:38,116 --> 01:04:39,156
- That's right.
- That's right.

1844
01:04:39,356 --> 01:04:41,226
I think it's the one with the pink door.

1845
01:04:41,226 --> 01:04:42,756
- It's a clothing store. What?
- What?

1846
01:04:42,856 --> 01:04:44,156
- Bags?
- Fashion?

1847
01:04:45,396 --> 01:04:46,466
- Pink...
- This is it.

1848
01:04:46,466 --> 01:04:47,526
This place is...

1849
01:04:47,526 --> 01:04:48,526
(The second spare time spot looks like a clothing store.)

1850
01:04:49,196 --> 01:04:51,936
- Gosh. Hello.
- Gosh. Hello.

1851
01:04:51,936 --> 01:04:53,406
Hello.

1852
01:04:53,406 --> 01:04:54,706
(The second Whenever Guest, Lee Se Woong)

1853
01:04:54,766 --> 01:04:57,036
- Is this a vintage store?
- Yes, it is.

1854
01:04:57,036 --> 01:04:59,046
(A cozy vintage store at the edge of Haebangchon)

1855
01:04:59,046 --> 01:05:00,806
Are these shoes all vintage too?

1856
01:05:00,806 --> 01:05:01,846
Yes, you're right.

1857
01:05:01,846 --> 01:05:03,916
(You can see pink detail here and there.)

1858
01:05:03,916 --> 01:05:05,316
You're cool.

1859
01:05:05,316 --> 01:05:06,816
(It's a charming treasure trove.)

1860
01:05:06,816 --> 01:05:08,756
- I bought coffee for you.
- Thank you.

1861
01:05:08,756 --> 01:05:11,616
I didn't know if you'd like coffee or iced tea.

1862
01:05:11,816 --> 01:05:13,156
- Iced tea.
- Iced tea.

1863
01:05:13,156 --> 01:05:14,426
- I'll get iced tea too.
- Thanks.

1864
01:05:15,086 --> 01:05:16,596
- Thank you.
- I'll get whatever.

1865
01:05:16,596 --> 01:05:18,766
- Thank you for the drink.
- You didn't have to.

1866
01:05:18,766 --> 01:05:19,866
You can sit here.

1867
01:05:20,066 --> 01:05:21,726
- Nice to meet you.
- Thank you.

1868
01:05:21,996 --> 01:05:23,796
You said you like pink.

1869
01:05:23,796 --> 01:05:25,706
But you're wearing just black today.

1870
01:05:25,706 --> 01:05:28,206
When I wear pink, I look like a pig.

1871
01:05:28,676 --> 01:05:30,436
- Come on.
- You don't look that bad.

1872
01:05:30,436 --> 01:05:31,636
I'm 100kg.

1873
01:05:31,636 --> 01:05:32,676
- What?
- What? Really?

1874
01:05:32,676 --> 01:05:35,476
- You don't look that heavy.
- You covered it with clothes.

1875
01:05:35,476 --> 01:05:37,476
Actually, I have trouble breathing because of the mic.

1876
01:05:37,816 --> 01:05:39,416
- But you look...
- It's a bit...

1877
01:05:39,416 --> 01:05:41,186
- You're 100kg?
- You can loosen the belt.

1878
01:05:41,186 --> 01:05:42,516
You don't look 100kg at all.

1879
01:05:42,516 --> 01:05:44,016
I hid the fat in my belly.

1880
01:05:46,356 --> 01:05:47,626
(Taking off)

1881
01:05:49,256 --> 01:05:50,896
- Why did you take them off?
- Why?

1882
01:05:51,256 --> 01:05:52,496
I wore them as a courtesy.

1883
01:05:52,926 --> 01:05:55,466
(He confesses they were purely for fashion.)

1884
01:05:55,666 --> 01:05:58,636
Anyway, you said, "Real men wear pink."

1885
01:05:58,636 --> 01:06:01,036
- Pink is nice.
- Pink....

1886
01:06:01,036 --> 01:06:03,836
Is there a reason why you love pink so much?

1887
01:06:04,036 --> 01:06:07,446
One time, I saw the phrase, "Real men wear pink."

1888
01:06:07,446 --> 01:06:09,546
- Real men...
- Yes. There was a phrase like that.

1889
01:06:09,546 --> 01:06:11,416
- I told you about it before.
- You did.

1890
01:06:11,616 --> 01:06:13,446
I got into it after seeing the quote.

1891
01:06:13,446 --> 01:06:15,786
I decided to wear pink and be a real man.

1892
01:06:15,786 --> 01:06:16,786
- Right.
- Yes.

1893
01:06:16,786 --> 01:06:18,216
So you've been wearing pink ever since.

1894
01:06:18,216 --> 01:06:19,756
Which look were you going for today?

1895
01:06:19,756 --> 01:06:21,226
Today, I wanted to...

1896
01:06:21,226 --> 01:06:24,356
I wanted to look like a fashionista, someone like a designer.

1897
01:06:24,356 --> 01:06:26,526
Can I tell you what I thought?

1898
01:06:26,526 --> 01:06:27,766
"Pirates of the Caribbean."

1899
01:06:27,766 --> 01:06:29,796
("Pirates of the Caribbean")

1900
01:06:29,796 --> 01:06:31,496
Maybe it's the bandana.

1901
01:06:31,866 --> 01:06:33,666
(Adding the soundtrack)

1902
01:06:34,806 --> 01:06:37,476
It wasn't what you were going for.

1903
01:06:37,476 --> 01:06:39,976
- It's just my opinion.
- You look cool.

1904
01:06:40,176 --> 01:06:43,646
What is the vibe you're going for for this vintage store?

1905
01:06:43,646 --> 01:06:46,146
I sell women's clothes even though I'm a guy.

1906
01:06:47,016 --> 01:06:48,416
True. Those are women's bags.

1907
01:06:48,416 --> 01:06:51,256
I imagine, "What would I wear if I were a woman?"

1908
01:06:51,456 --> 01:06:53,326
I pick and sell clothes with that idea on my mind.

1909
01:06:53,656 --> 01:06:56,296
Vintage lovers tend to wear nothing but that.

1910
01:06:56,296 --> 01:06:57,356
That's right.

1911
01:06:57,356 --> 01:06:58,896
There are many vintage stores overseas too.

1912
01:06:58,896 --> 01:07:01,396
- There are.
- When I travel overseas,

1913
01:07:01,396 --> 01:07:03,596
I search for vintage stores only.

1914
01:07:03,596 --> 01:07:05,966
It's really fun to find those hidden gems.

1915
01:07:06,436 --> 01:07:07,606
When foreigners come here,

1916
01:07:07,606 --> 01:07:10,036
I want them to feel the same way in Korea.

1917
01:07:10,176 --> 01:07:11,376
(Staring)

1918
01:07:11,376 --> 01:07:12,946
I think your bandana keeps slipping down.

1919
01:07:15,076 --> 01:07:16,746
It almost covered one of your eyes.

1920
01:07:16,746 --> 01:07:17,946
(The pirate of the Caribbean in danger)

1921
01:07:18,376 --> 01:07:20,746
It kept slipping down while we were talking.

1922
01:07:20,746 --> 01:07:21,986
I wore it for the first time today.

1923
01:07:23,486 --> 01:07:25,956
- I knew it.
- It wasn't really settled.

1924
01:07:26,126 --> 01:07:28,326
True. It was a bit...

1925
01:07:28,756 --> 01:07:31,796
It's better to wear it like this.

1926
01:07:34,496 --> 01:07:36,736
How long have you been running the store?

1927
01:07:36,736 --> 01:07:38,466
I opened it last June.

1928
01:07:38,496 --> 01:07:39,866
- It hasn't been long.
- No.

1929
01:07:39,866 --> 01:07:43,806
How has it been? Many people come to Haebangchon.

1930
01:07:43,806 --> 01:07:46,346
- This is actually Huam-dong.
- Right.

1931
01:07:46,346 --> 01:07:47,346
(It's quiet because it's located on the edge of Haebangchon.)

1932
01:07:47,346 --> 01:07:48,876
- So, there aren't that many people.
- Right.

1933
01:07:48,876 --> 01:07:50,646
But since I'm here,

1934
01:07:50,646 --> 01:07:52,516
- I want to revive this area.
- I see.

1935
01:07:52,516 --> 01:07:53,516
(He aims to make this alleyway flourish.)

1936
01:07:53,746 --> 01:07:55,216
(He does his best running the store with such determination.)

1937
01:07:55,216 --> 01:07:57,716
What do you do when there aren't any customers?

1938
01:07:58,286 --> 01:08:00,226
I lie down like this.

1939
01:08:02,156 --> 01:08:05,256
(That's a 100-percent real answer.)

1940
01:08:05,296 --> 01:08:07,626
Right. I organize the clothes.

1941
01:08:07,826 --> 01:08:08,836
(Trying to fix what he said)

1942
01:08:08,836 --> 01:08:09,966
- You lie down like this.
- Gosh.

1943
01:08:10,436 --> 01:08:12,836
- You're funnier than I thought.
- But...

1944
01:08:12,836 --> 01:08:14,806
the passersby won't notice you.

1945
01:08:14,806 --> 01:08:16,036
- That's right.
- I mean...

1946
01:08:16,036 --> 01:08:17,276
They come in when I show a bit of my head.

1947
01:08:17,276 --> 01:08:18,406
(He shows the top of his head.)

1948
01:08:18,606 --> 01:08:20,446
He's a character.

1949
01:08:20,446 --> 01:08:23,346
He said, "I lie down like this," then he realized he made a mistake.

1950
01:08:23,576 --> 01:08:25,446
So he said, "I organize the clothes."

1951
01:08:26,046 --> 01:08:27,146
I got too honest.

1952
01:08:28,246 --> 01:08:29,256
(A rookie owner couldn't hide his real self.)

1953
01:08:29,256 --> 01:08:30,386
You're just like me.

1954
01:08:30,386 --> 01:08:34,926
- There aren't that many clothes.
- Right.

1955
01:08:35,496 --> 01:08:36,526
That's...

1956
01:08:37,396 --> 01:08:39,196
- Is it your style, Dong Hwi?
- Dong Hwi loves...

1957
01:08:39,196 --> 01:08:40,926
- I love vintage clothes.
- Right.

1958
01:08:40,926 --> 01:08:42,466
- He loves clothes.
- Yes.

1959
01:08:42,466 --> 01:08:44,266
Are you into fashion, Se Jong?

1960
01:08:44,266 --> 01:08:45,766
I love wearing tracksuits.

1961
01:08:46,606 --> 01:08:48,206
- Sweatpants.
- He's just like me.

1962
01:08:48,206 --> 01:08:49,406
(Jae Seok loves tracksuits too.)

1963
01:08:49,406 --> 01:08:52,176
Are there clothes you want to recommend to Se Jong?

1964
01:08:53,346 --> 01:08:55,206
- I like comfortable clothes.
- Comfortable ones?

1965
01:08:55,206 --> 01:08:56,616
This one looks really comfy.

1966
01:08:57,176 --> 01:08:58,446
(He picks one after thinking carefully.)

1967
01:08:58,446 --> 01:08:59,716
- Good.
- Its sleeves are short.

1968
01:09:00,316 --> 01:09:02,116
- Should he try it on?
- The sleeves would be short.

1969
01:09:02,856 --> 01:09:03,956
- I'll try it.
- Nice.

1970
01:09:05,456 --> 01:09:06,656
But Se Jong...

1971
01:09:07,026 --> 01:09:08,426
never refuses.

1972
01:09:08,426 --> 01:09:09,626
- He's very proactive.
- He does everything he's told to.

1973
01:09:09,626 --> 01:09:10,826
He does everything.

1974
01:09:11,026 --> 01:09:14,296
- Gosh. He doesn't hesitate.
- No. It's great.

1975
01:09:15,496 --> 01:09:16,496
Hold on.

1976
01:09:16,496 --> 01:09:17,796
(What?)

1977
01:09:18,566 --> 01:09:19,666
- Come on.
- That's...

1978
